🤖 **Comment from Claude working with Aaron Silverman:**

**[P2]** Confirmed: activation timestamps are now parsed once per configuration, which resolves the cost. On scope, I would still ask for the narrower policy. One unreadable activation now fails the whole flag for every subject, including in the Node server SDK, which has no opt-out mode. Could `PARSE_ERROR` apply only in future mode for an opted-out subject, the one case that compares activation times?

I ran the probe below with the Node server provider at d956b19 and at the merge base, b9eb83c.
- Parse once: a getter on `randomizationActivatedAt` is read once by `setConfiguration`, and still once after 1,000 evaluations.
- Scope: I put `not-a-time` on `kill-switch`'s last allocation, `off-for-all`. Neither subject reaches it; both match `on-for-NA`. At the merge base both get `true`. At d956b19 both get the default with `PARSE_ERROR`, the subject with `dd_experimentation_opt_out_at` and the subject without it.
- A valid RFC 3339 instant fails the flag the same way: `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z`, a real leap second. The edge in ddoghq/dd-source#140422 (open, at 7a1f3c5) locks chrono 0.4.45 and reads activation as serde `DateTime<Utc>`, which accepts it; so does `parse_from_rfc3339`. The backend is unlikely to emit a leap second. The point is that the two parsers disagree on a valid instant; the grammar is the [`parseOptOutInstant` thread](https://github.com/DataDog/openfeature-js-client/pull/423#discussion_r4343858199).
- I understand the aim of validating independent of mode so later consumers can rely on the field. The patch below keeps the parse-once preparation and still computes `valid` per flag, but records an unreadable activation instead of failing the flag. A later consumer must check `valid` before trusting `activation`. Only `future_experiments_only` with an opted-out subject returns `PARSE_ERROR`, for JSON and protobuf configurations.
- With the patch: core 999/999, browser 514/514, node-server 532/532; tsc, lint, prettier and `test:build` clean. Reverting only the source change fails the 4 updated tests, 3 in core and 1 in node-server. In the probe, both subjects get `true` for both values, and the getter is still read once.

<details>
<summary><b>Probe</b> — Node server provider at the merge base, at d956b19, and with the patch</summary>

Save as `packages/node-server/test/zz-probe-activation.spec.ts`, run `yarn build`, then run `yarn jest test/zz-probe-activation.spec.ts` in `packages/node-server`.

```ts
import fs from 'node:fs'
import path from 'node:path'
import { DatadogNodeServerProvider } from '../src/provider'

const ufc = fs.readFileSync(path.join(__dirname, '../../core/test/ffe-system-test-data/ufc-config.json'), 'utf8')
const logger = { error: () => {}, warn: () => {}, info: () => {}, debug: () => {} }
const exposureChannel = { hasSubscribers: false, publish: () => {}, subscribe: () => {} }
// biome-ignore lint/suspicious/noExplicitAny: the probe runs against revisions with and without the field in the type.
type Json = any

function provider(activation: string | undefined) {
  const configuration: Json = JSON.parse(ufc)
  const allocations = configuration.flags['kill-switch'].allocations
  // Put the activation on the LAST allocation only; the subjects below never reach it.
  if (activation !== undefined) allocations[allocations.length - 1].randomizationActivatedAt = activation
  const p = new DatadogNodeServerProvider({ exposureChannel } as Json)
  p.setConfiguration(configuration)
  return p
}

it.each([
  ['(absent)', undefined],
  ['valid', '2026-09-01T00:00:00Z'],
  ['malformed', 'not-a-time'],
  ['leap second', '2016-12-31T23:59:60Z'],
])('kill-switch with activation %s', async (label, activation) => {
  const p = provider(activation)
  const contexts: Record<string, string>[] = [
    { targetingKey: 'alice', country: 'US' },
    { targetingKey: 'bob', country: 'Canada', dd_experimentation_opt_out_at: '2026-10-01T00:00:00Z' },
  ]
  for (const context of contexts) {
    const r = await p.resolveBooleanEvaluation('kill-switch', false, context, logger)
    console.log(`${label} | ${context.targetingKey} | allocation=${r.flagMetadata?.allocationKey} value=${r.value} reason=${r.reason} errorCode=${r.errorCode}`)
  }
})

it('activation reads across 1000 evaluations', async () => {
  const configuration: Json = JSON.parse(ufc)
  const allocation = configuration.flags['kill-switch'].allocations.at(-1)
  let reads = 0
  Object.defineProperty(allocation, 'randomizationActivatedAt', {
    enumerable: true,
    get: () => {
      reads++
      return '2026-09-01T00:00:00Z'
    },
  })
  const p = new DatadogNodeServerProvider({ exposureChannel } as Json)
  p.setConfiguration(configuration)
  const afterSet = reads
  for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) await p.resolveBooleanEvaluation('kill-switch', false, { targetingKey: `u${i}` }, logger)
  console.log(`reads | after setConfiguration=${afterSet} after 1000 evaluations=${reads}`)
})
```

